SwirlOS syscall ABI (v1)
========================

Ring-3 code requests kernel services with:

    int 0x80

Arguments follow the SysV register convention:

    rax = syscall number
    rdi, rsi, rdx = arguments 1..3
    return value in rax; negative value (int32) = error code

This ABI belongs to SwirlOS. It is deliberately not the Linux ABI: there
is no fork, no signals, no mmap. File descriptors are small integers.

  #   name        args                returns
  --  ----------  ------------------  -----------------------------
  0   open        path, flags         fd     flags: 1=read 2=write 4=create 8=truncate
  1   read        fd, buf, len        bytes read (0 = EOF)
  2   write       fd, buf, len        bytes written
  3   close       fd                  0
  4   seek        fd, off, whence     new offset (whence: 0=set 1=cur 2=end)
  5   dir_open    path                handle (>2), or negative on error
  6   dir_read    handle, buf         1 and fills buf{name[56],type} or 0 at end
  7   spawn       path                pid of the new ring-3 process
  8   wait        pid                 exit code of that child
  9   exit        code                does not return
  10  getpid      -                   pid
  11  ppid        -                   parent pid
  12  sysinfo     buf                 fills sysinfo struct (see libswirl)
  13  fb_info     buf                 {u32 width, height, bpp}
  14  fb_rect     x,y,w,h,color       fill rectangle on the framebuffer
  15  fb_text     x,y,str,size,color  draw text with the system font
  16  key_read    -                   next keypress (unicode-ish byte), blocks
  17  mkdir       path                0
  18  unlink      path                0 (files and empty directories)
  19  proc_list   buf, max            number of packed proc records read
  20  getring     -                   current privilege ring (3)
  21  fb_scroll   lines               scroll the console area up

Error codes: -1 bad args, -2 not found, -3 no access, -4 exists,
             -5 out of resources, -6 not a directory, -7 is a directory,
             -8 would block (should not happen: reads block)

